# Local Environment

**URL:** `https://rad.localdev/`
**Login:** username `test` / password `testtest`

## Docker on Apple Silicon

The ECR `lsr-*-toolbox:*-latest` image manifests have no `linux/arm64/v8` variant. On Apple Silicon, export `DOCKER_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=linux/amd64` (in `~/.zshrc` or per-invocation) so Docker pulls the amd64 layer and runs it under Rosetta. Symptom without it: `no matching manifest for linux/arm64/v8 in the manifest list entries`. Affects every `make` target that pulls an image (`make up`, `make provision-*`, `make -C cli/tester php-test …`, etc.).

## UI Navigation

### Login

- Go to `https://rad.localdev/`
- Fill username and password, click Sign In
- After login, lands on the **System** tab
- Do NOT screenshot the login page — it has no value as evidence

### Settings > Item Fees

1. Click **Settings** in the top navigation bar
2. Under **PRICING**, click the **Item Fees** tile
3. Item Fees listing: `https://rad.localdev/settings/item-fees/`
4. Click **Add item fee** to create a new fee: `https://rad.localdev/settings/item-fees/new`
5. Click an existing fee name to open its detail page: `https://rad.localdev/settings/item-fees/{id}`

### System > Modules (feature flag management)

1. Click **System** in the top navigation bar
2. Click **Modules** under "Account Management"
3. Modules listing: `https://rad.localdev/?name=system.listings.modules&form_name=listing`

#### Searching for a module

- Use the **Name** text field at the top, then click **Search**
- If "No system modules were found." appears, the module does not exist yet

#### Creating a new module

1. Click **New Module**
2. Fill the **Name** field with the module name (e.g. `item_fees.percent`)
3. Leave **State** as `development`
4. Ignore other fields
5. Click **Save Changes**

#### Enabling a module for an account

1. Open the module detail page (search for it first, then click the module name)
2. In the **Accounts** section at the bottom:
   - Fill **Account ID** (e.g. `1`)
   - Check **"Doesn't have module"**
   - Click **Search** — this lists accounts that do NOT have the module
3. Click **Add Module** — confirms with a dialog "Are you sure you want to bulk add this module to selected accounts?"
4. Accept the dialog
5. The MODULE column will now show **"Yes"** for the account

#### Disabling a module for an account

1. Open the module detail page
2. In the **Accounts** section:
   - Fill **Account ID**
   - Leave "Doesn't have module" **unchecked**
   - Click **Search** — this lists accounts that HAVE the module
3. Click **Remove Module** and confirm

## API Usage

### Item Fee API

**Base URL:** `/API/V3/Account/{accountID}/ItemFee.json`

**Authentication:** Session cookie (already authenticated when using `evaluate_script` from the browser) or basic auth with `test`/`testtest`.

**Input format:** JSON (when using the `.json` endpoint). Flat object — no wrapper element.

**Examples:**

Create:
```json
POST /API/V3/Account/1/ItemFee.json
{"name": "10% Fee", "calculationMethod": "percentage", "feeValue": "0.10", "taxable": "false"}
```

Update:
```json
PUT /API/V3/Account/1/ItemFee/{itemFeeID}.json
{"feeValue": "0.05"}
```

Read all:
```
GET /API/V3/Account/1/ItemFee.json
```

## Taking Screenshots for PR Evidence

- Use the Chrome DevTools MCP tools: `navigate_page`, `take_snapshot`, `fill`, `click`, `wait_for`, `take_screenshot`, `handle_dialog`
- Always call `take_snapshot` before interacting with elements (to get UIDs)
- For backend-only changes where the UI doesn't yet expose the feature, use **API calls** via `evaluate_script` and display results in the browser, then screenshot
- For dialogs (confirm/alert), use `handle_dialog` with `{"action": "accept"}` or `{"action": "dismiss"}`
- **Always save screenshots to `~/Pictures/cursorScreenshots/`** — never to the project directory or any other location
